---
title: Overview
description: Architecture and building blocks of Nimbus Sync.
---

# Overview

{% $product %} follows a classic client–server architecture. Clients detect changes in the file system, transfer only the blocks that have changed and receive changes from other devices in real time.

{% figure src="../assets/architecture.svg" caption="Nimbus Sync architecture" alt="Clients connect through the gateway to the sync service, the metadata database and object storage." /%}

## Building blocks

| Component | Responsibility | Technology |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Gateway | Authentication, authorization, load balancing | Rust, HTTP/2 |
| Sync service | Merges changes, detects conflicts | Rust |
| Metadata | Folder structure, versions, shares | PostgreSQL 16 |
| Object storage | File contents in encrypted blocks | S3-compatible |

## Synchronization

Every file is split into blocks of 4 MiB. When a file changes, the client uploads only the affected blocks. The storage a versioned folder needs can be estimated as follows:

```math
S approx s dot (1 + v dot r)
```

Here {% math formula="s" /%} is the size of the current data, {% math formula="v" /%} the number of versions retained and {% math formula="r" /%} the average share of data that changes per version.

{% callout type="important" title="End-to-end encryption" %}
File contents are encrypted with AES-256-GCM on the device itself. Neither the server nor the storage provider can read them. Key management is described in [Configuration › Encryption](configuration.mdoc#encryption).
{% /callout %}

## Conflicts

When two people edit the same file at the same time, no change is lost: {% $product %} creates a conflicted copy and notifies both of them.

{% details title="How is a conflict detected?" %}
Every block carries a version vector. If the client's vector no longer matches the server's when it uploads, there is a conflict. The conflicted copy gets the suffix `(conflict <device> <date>)`.
{% /details %}
